# Local environment overrides — the tracked shape of an untracked file.
#
# Copy this file to `.env` and fill in the values for your own robot and
# network. `.env`, and every `.env.*` beside it, is ignored by version control;
# this example is the only tracked copy, and it never holds a real value. That
# split is the whole point: identifiers belonging to a deployment environment —
# hostnames, addresses, accounts, credentials — do not enter a public
# repository, and the automated leak scan rejects them if they try.
#
# Anything shown here as an example uses an address from an RFC 5737
# documentation range or a name under `example.com`. Those are reserved for
# exactly this purpose and the leak scan lets them through by shape.
#
#     ROBOT_ADDRESS=192.0.2.10
#     MAINTAINER_CONTACT=ops@example.com
#
# Shape
# -----
# One `NAME=value` per line, no quoting, no interpolation. Every variable is
# prefixed with the component that reads it, so what a value affects is legible
# without consulting the component — the groundstation's are
# `REACHY_GROUNDSTATION_*`, and each other component's change document names its
# own prefix as it lands.
#
# A component fails to start on a variable that matches its own prefix and that
# it does not recognise, so a typo here is reported rather than silently
# ignored, and it emits its fully resolved configuration at startup with every
# secret shown as set or unset rather than by value.
#
# Each component's change document adds its variables to this file as it lands,
# so a variable absent from here is a variable nothing reads.
#
# The groundstation
# -----------------
# The only variable without a default is the credential, and the service refuses
# to start without it: a groundstation that authenticated nothing because nobody
# configured it is a worse failure than one that will not start. Everything else
# below is shown at its default, so this section doubles as the list of what the
# service resolves — the same list its startup log and its `/config` endpoint
# report.
#
# Every line here is commented out. Uncomment the ones you are changing in your
# own `.env`; a variable left commented is a variable at its default, and a
# variable misspelled is a startup failure that names it.
#
#     REACHY_GROUNDSTATION_CREDENTIAL=replace-with-your-own-shared-secret
#
#     REACHY_GROUNDSTATION_HOST=127.0.0.1
#     REACHY_GROUNDSTATION_PORT=8080
#     REACHY_GROUNDSTATION_QUEUE_BOUND=2
#     REACHY_GROUNDSTATION_CAPABILITY_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=5.0
#     REACHY_GROUNDSTATION_HANDSHAKE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=10.0
#     REACHY_GROUNDSTATION_WARM_UP_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=60.0
#     REACHY_GROUNDSTATION_MAX_MESSAGE_BYTES=4194304
#     REACHY_GROUNDSTATION_LOG_LEVEL=info
#     REACHY_GROUNDSTATION_LOG_FORMAT=json
#     REACHY_GROUNDSTATION_SERVICE_NAME=reachy-groundstation
#
# Perception. Models are fetched and hash-verified when the artifact is built
# and are read from the directory below at run time; the service never fetches
# weights over the network. The default below is where the container image bakes
# them; `just models` writes to `.models/` at the root of a checkout, so a local
# run sets the variable to that instead:
#
#     REACHY_GROUNDSTATION_MODELS_DIR=/opt/reachy/models
#     REACHY_GROUNDSTATION_MODELS_DIR=.models
#
# Inference parallelism is configuration rather than something the runtime sizes
# against the host. Four intra-operator threads is where the predecessor's
# measured curve flattened — 93 ms at one, 51 ms at four, 55 ms at six. The
# provider list is an ordered preference; anything this build has not got is
# skipped, and the accelerated image variant is the reason it is a list:
#
#     REACHY_GROUNDSTATION_INFERENCE_INTRA_OP_THREADS=4
#     REACHY_GROUNDSTATION_INFERENCE_INTER_OP_THREADS=1
#     REACHY_GROUNDSTATION_INFERENCE_PROVIDERS=CPUExecutionProvider
#
# Each detector is switched on or off independently, and switching one off
# leaves the others serving. Gestures are off by default because no gesture
# model clears this repository's licence and provenance bar yet, so the
# capability ships with no model wired:
#
#     REACHY_GROUNDSTATION_FACE_ENABLED=true
#     REACHY_GROUNDSTATION_FACE_SCORE_THRESHOLD=0.6
#     REACHY_GROUNDSTATION_FACE_NMS_THRESHOLD=0.3
#     REACHY_GROUNDSTATION_GESTURE_ENABLED=false
#     REACHY_GROUNDSTATION_GESTURE_SCORE_THRESHOLD=0.6
#     REACHY_GROUNDSTATION_GESTURE_SAMPLE_INTERVAL=4
#
# The HA satellite
# ----------------
# The only variable without a default is the announced device name, and the
# application refuses to start without it. That is deliberate and it is the one
# migration hazard in the project: Home Assistant keys an ESPHome device on the
# identity it announces, so changing it registers a NEW device — every entity
# gains a suffixed identifier, history detaches, and every automation and
# dashboard card referencing the old identifiers silently stops matching. A
# default derived from the package name would be correct on a fresh install and
# silently destructive on the upgrade that actually exists.
#
# Upgrading: set it to whatever the previous application announced. Home
# Assistant shows it on the device page. New robot: choose one and never change
# it. Only the ANNOUNCED name is dangerous — the friendly name is a display name
# and is safe to change.
#
# See docs/ops/satellite-deployment.md, which explains all of this at length.
#
#     REACHY_SATELLITE_DEVICE_NAME=reachy-mini-1
#
# Home Assistant also keys the device on the hardware address. Left unset it is
# read from the network interface at startup and reported in the boot log and on
# the settings page; pin it when the robot's network hardware has changed:
#
#     REACHY_SATELLITE_FRIENDLY_NAME=Reachy Mini
#     REACHY_SATELLITE_MAC_ADDRESS=02:00:5e:10:00:00
#     REACHY_SATELLITE_NETWORK_INTERFACE=wlan0
#
# Where the ESPHome native API and the settings interface bind. 6053 is the port
# Home Assistant looks for; the mDNS advertisement is how it discovers the robot
# without being told where it is.
#
# The three WEB_ variables are read from the environment only, and the settings
# page shows them without offering to change them. An override sits above the
# environment, so a page that had switched itself off — or moved itself to an
# address nothing can reach — would be the page you could no longer open to undo
# it:
#
#     REACHY_SATELLITE_API_HOST=0.0.0.0
#     REACHY_SATELLITE_API_PORT=6053
#     REACHY_SATELLITE_ADVERTISE=true
#     REACHY_SATELLITE_WEB_ENABLED=true
#     REACHY_SATELLITE_WEB_HOST=0.0.0.0
#     REACHY_SATELLITE_WEB_PORT=8088
#
# Everything that outlives the wheel — preferences Home Assistant sets through
# entities, downloaded media, and the settings the web interface writes. The
# state directory is the one setting the web interface shows and does not write:
# it names the directory that interface's own file lives in, so an override for
# it would be a file saying where to look for itself.
#
#     REACHY_SATELLITE_STATE_DIR=~/.local/state/reachy-mini-ha-satellite
#     REACHY_SATELLITE_ACTIVE_WAKE_WORD=okay_nabu
#     REACHY_SATELLITE_SAMPLES_PER_CHUNK=160
#
# Face tracking. Remote is the default and the reason is measured: with detection
# offloaded the predecessor's robot sat at 1.52 of its four cores, and with
# detection local it saturated. The local detector's weights are NOT shipped in
# the wheel, so `local` and `remote_with_local_fallback` both need a path to a
# model file on the robot; `remote` and `remote_with_local_fallback` both need an
# address and a credential. The application refuses to start if the selection
# needs something it has not been given.
#
# The address carries no credential: user information before the @, a query and a
# fragment are all refused. It is not a secret setting, so it is printed in the
# boot log and shown on the settings page, and no redactor can remove a value it
# was never given. The credential has a setting of its own, and that one is never
# printed.
#
# The address is also capped at 255 characters, which is the most a Home
# Assistant text entity can report and is why the satellite announces it as one.
# An installation carrying a longer address from a release that allowed 512 is
# refused at startup rather than truncated, with a message naming the layer that
# holds it — see docs/ops/satellite-deployment.md. Unlike the rest of this
# group, the address applies at once: the settings page and Home Assistant both
# change it without a restart.
#
#     REACHY_SATELLITE_FACE_TRACKING_ENABLED=true
#     REACHY_SATELLITE_DETECTION_SOURCE=remote
#     REACHY_SATELLITE_GROUNDSTATION_URL=ws://192.0.2.10:8080/v1/session
#     REACHY_SATELLITE_GROUNDSTATION_CREDENTIAL=replace-with-your-own-shared-secret
#     REACHY_SATELLITE_FRAME_INTERVAL_SECONDS=0.1
#     REACHY_SATELLITE_STALENESS_SECONDS=2.0
#     REACHY_SATELLITE_LOCAL_MODEL_PATH=/opt/reachy/models/face_detection.onnx
#     REACHY_SATELLITE_LOCAL_SCORE_THRESHOLD=0.6
#     REACHY_SATELLITE_LOCAL_NMS_THRESHOLD=0.3
#     REACHY_SATELLITE_LOCAL_DETECTION_INTERVAL_SECONDS=0.2
#
# How a normalised detection becomes an angle, and how the head follows it:
#
#     REACHY_SATELLITE_CAMERA_HORIZONTAL_FOV_DEGREES=87.0
#     REACHY_SATELLITE_CAMERA_VERTICAL_FOV_DEGREES=67.0
#     REACHY_SATELLITE_BEHAVIOUR_TICK_SECONDS=0.05
#     REACHY_SATELLITE_GAZE_DEADZONE=0.02
#     REACHY_SATELLITE_GAZE_SMOOTHING=0.35
#     REACHY_SATELLITE_IDLE_SECONDS=6.0
#     REACHY_SATELLITE_LOG_LEVEL=info
